  "question": {
    "text": "In a radioactive decay chain, the initial nucleus is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>90</mn><mn>232</mn></msubsup><mi>Th</mi></math>. At the end, there are <math><mn>6</mn></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-particles and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>&#946;</mi></math>-particles which are emitted. If the end nucleus is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mrow/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>A</mi></math> and <math><mi>Z</mi></math> are given by:",
